      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Data on the contemporary mortality of coronary heart disease (CHD) are surprisingly sparse."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To describe the contemporary mortality of all manifestations of CHD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Prospective follow-up of patients with a first presentation of CHD in a defined population."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "We studied 537 patients with a first presentation of acute myocardial infarction, unstable angina or new exertional angina in Bromley Health Authority, London (population 295,000). Patients were prospectively monitored for cardiac and non-cardiac mortality for a median of 6 years."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "During a median 6 years follow-up, there were 88 (16%) deaths. Survival free from cardiac death was not significantly different between unstable angina (92%) and new exertional angina (94%), but was lower for acute myocardial infarction (84%)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION",
          "content": "Mortality from CHD appears to be falling. However, efforts to prevent myocardial infarction should continue to be a priority, because on-going early mortality remains high. New exertional angina should be diagnosed and managed promptly, as its mortality is similar to that of unstable angina."
        }
      ]},
